(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Objective: To compare efficacy and tolerability of a cosmetic acne regimen with) Tj
T*
(prescription therapy in diverse populations. Design: Single-center, double-blind trial.) Tj
T*
(Participants: 54 adults \(31 cosmetic regimen, 23 prescription regimen\), predominantly) Tj
T*
(female \(90.7%\), diverse ethnic backgrounds. Intervention: Cosmetic salicylic acid system) Tj
T*
(versus prescription retinoid-peroxide combination applied to face for 12 weeks. Results:) Tj
T*
(Clinician-assessed acne severity improved significantly from baseline in both groups) Tj
T*
(beginning at 48 hours and sustained through 12 weeks \(p<=0.001\). Total facial lesion counts) Tj
T*
(declined similarly in both treatment arms. Post-inflammatory changes and facial oiliness) Tj
T*
(showed comparable improvement. Notably, the cosmetic regimen demonstrated significantly) Tj
T*
(better skin tolerability scores at week 4 for skin tightness and dryness sensations) Tj
T*
(\(p=0.008\). Patient satisfaction exceeded 96% with cosmetic regimen across all timepoints) Tj
T*
(versus lower satisfaction with prescription therapy due to irritation concerns. Both) Tj
T*
(regimens were well-tolerated overall, with no serious adverse events. Conclusions: The) Tj
T*
(cosmetic anti-acne regimen achieved comparable efficacy to prescription therapy with) Tj
T*
(superior tolerability in patients with mild-to-moderate acne.) Tj
